2026-10-20T21:06:14.4360647Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4361200Z +             text: 'Including surplus keyword arguments in a formatting call makes code more difficult to read and may indicate an error.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4361754Z -             text: 'Defining an attribute in a superclass method with a name that matches a subclass method, hides the method in the subclass.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4361838Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4361928Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4362175Z +             markdown: '# Unused named argument in formatting call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4364183Z +               'A formatting expression, that is an expression of the form `the_format.format(args)` or `format(the_format, args)` can have keyword arguments of any name, as long as all the required names are provided. However, surplus keyword arguments, those with names that are not in the format, are redundant. These surplus arguments clutter the code, making it harder to read.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4364489Z -             markdown: '# Superclass attribute shadows subclass method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4366273Z -               "When an object has an attribute that shares its name with a method on the object's class (or another class attribute), the instance attribute is prioritized during attribute lookup, shadowing the method. If a method on a subclass is shadowed by an attribute on a superclass in this way, this may lead to unexpected results or errors, as this shadowing behavior is nonlocal and may be unintended.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4366365Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4366796Z +               'It is also possible that surplus keyword arguments indicate a mistake in the format string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4366878Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4366977Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4367108Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4367503Z +               'Check that the format string is correct and then remove any surplus keyword arguments.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4368480Z -               "Ensure method names on subclasses don't conflict with attribute names on superclasses, and rename one. If the shadowing behavior is intended, ensure this is explicit in the superclass.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4368568Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4368648Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4368765Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4369349Z +               'In the following example, the comment indicates that the `chips` keyword argument is no longer required and should be deleted.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370134Z -               'In the following example, the `_foo` attribute of class `A` shadows the method `_foo` of class `B`. Calls to `B()._foo()` will result in a `TypeError`, as `3` will be called instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370226Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370312Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370420Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370582Z +               'def surplus_argument():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4370885Z +               '    the_format = "{spam} {eggs}" # Used to be "{spam} {eggs} {chips}"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371221Z +               '    return the_format.format(spam = "spam", eggs="eggs", chips="chips")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371350Z -               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371500Z -               '    def __init__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371649Z -               '        self._foo = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371733Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4371862Z -               'class B(A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372085Z -               '    # BAD: _foo is shadowed by attribute A._foo\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372231Z -               '    def _foo(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372364Z -               '        return 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372465Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372557Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4372646Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4373678Z -               'In the following example, the behavior of the `default` attribute being shadowed to allow for customization during initialization is intended in within the superclass `A`. Overriding `default` in the subclass `B` is then OK.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4373758Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4374185Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4374735Z +               '* Python Library Reference: [String Formatting.](https://docs.python.org/2/library/string.html#string-formatting)\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4374965Z +             text: '# Unused named argument in formatting call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4376785Z +               'A formatting expression, that is an expression of the form `the_format.format(args)` or `format(the_format, args)` can have keyword arguments of any name, as long as all the required names are provided. However, surplus keyword arguments, those with names that are not in the format, are redundant. These surplus arguments clutter the code, making it harder to read.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4376885Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4377307Z +               'It is also possible that surplus keyword arguments indicate a mistake in the format string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4377438Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4377564Z -               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4377766Z -               '    def __init__(self, default_func=None):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4377954Z -               '        if default_func is not None:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378151Z -               '            self.default = default_func \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378230Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378574Z -               '    # GOOD: The shadowing behavior is explicitly intended in the superclass.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378735Z -               '    def default(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378868Z -               '        return []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4378973Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4379102Z -               'class B(A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4379327Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4379870Z -               '    # Subclasses may override the method `default`, which will still be shadowed by the attribute `default` if it is set.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4380219Z -               '    # As this is part of the expected behavior of the superclass, this is fine. \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4380373Z -               '    def default(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4380511Z -               '        return {}\n' +
