2026-10-20T21:06:14.4586496Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4586685Z +             markdown: '# Statement has no effect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4587329Z +               'An expression statement without side effects is just clutter. It confuses the reader and may have a slight impact on performance.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4587579Z -             markdown: '# Inconsistent method resolution order\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4590025Z -               'Python 2.3 introduced new-style classes (classes inheriting from object). New-style classes use the C3 linearization method to determine a method resolution ordering (MRO) for each class. The C3 linearization method ensures that for a class C, if a class C1 precedes a class C2 in the MRO of C then C1 should also precede C2 in the MRO of all subclasses of C. It is possible to create a situation where it is impossible to achieve this consistency and this will guarantee that a ` TypeError` will be raised at runtime.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4590120Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4590211Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4590339Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4591353Z +               'First determine what the intention of the code was, if there is no intention of a side effect, then just delete the statement. However, it is probable that there is a mistake in the code and some effect was intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4591581Z -               'Use a class hierarchy that is not ambiguous.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4591664Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4593207Z +               'This query will not flag a statement consisting solely of a string as having no side effect, as these are often used as comments. If you want to use strings as comments, the most common convention is to use triple quoted strings rather than single quoted ones. Although consistency is more important than conforming to any particular style.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4593293Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4593383Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4593502Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4594033Z +               'In this example neither line of the `increment_and_show()` function has any effect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4594949Z -               'The MRO of class `X` is just `X, object`. The program will fail when the MRO of class `Y` needs to be calculated because `object` precedes `X` in the definition of `Y` but the opposite is true in the MRO of `X`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4595037Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4595582Z +               'The first line, `++x`, has no effect as it applies the unary plus operator twice. Probably the programmer intended `x += 1`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4595664Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596240Z +               'The second line, `x.show`, has no observable effect, but it is likely that `x.show()` was intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596342Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596436Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596551Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596697Z -               'class X(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596851Z -               '    def __init__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4596995Z -               '        print("X")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597133Z -               'class Y(object,X):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597289Z -               '    def __init__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597427Z -               '        print("Y")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597522Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597609Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597772Z +               'def increment_and_show(x):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597872Z +               '    ++x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4597997Z +               '    x.show\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4598124Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4598593Z -               '* Python: [The Python 2.3 Method Resolution Order](http://www.python.org/download/releases/2.3/mro/).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4598808Z -             text: '# Inconsistent method resolution order\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4601250Z -               'Python 2.3 introduced new-style classes (classes inheriting from object). New-style classes use the C3 linearization method to determine a method resolution ordering (MRO) for each class. The C3 linearization method ensures that for a class C, if a class C1 precedes a class C2 in the MRO of C then C1 should also precede C2 in the MRO of all subclasses of C. It is possible to create a situation where it is impossible to achieve this consistency and this will guarantee that a ` TypeError` will be raised at runtime.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4601333Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4601434Z +               '```\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4601610Z +             text: '# Statement has no effect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4602202Z +               'An expression statement without side effects is just clutter. It confuses the reader and may have a slight impact on performance.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4602287Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4602378Z +               '\n' +
