2026-10-20T21:06:14.4684603Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4685198Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4685684Z +               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4685991Z +             text: '# Nested loops with same variable reused after inner loop body\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4688221Z +               'In Python variables have function-wide scope which means that if two variables have the same name in the same scope, they are in fact one variable. Consequently, nested loops in which the target variables have the same name in fact share a single variable. Such loops are difficult to understand as the inner loop will modify the target variable of the outer loop. This may lead to unexpected behavior if the loop variable is used after the inner loop has terminated.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4688692Z -               '* Python Glossary: [New-style class](http://docs.python.org/glossary.html#term-new-style-class).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4689260Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [New-style and classic classes](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel.html#newstyle).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4689673Z -               '* Python Standard Library: [super](http://docs.python.org/library/functions.html#super).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4689856Z -             text: "# 'super' in old style class\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4690756Z -               'The ability to access inherited methods that have been overridden in a class using `super()` is supported only by new-style classes. When you use the `super()` function in an old-style class it fails.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4691004Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4691091Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4691210Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4691390Z +               'Rename the inner loop variable.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4692896Z -               'If you want to access inherited methods using the `super()` built-in, then ensure that the class is a new-style class. You can convert an old-style class to a new-style class by inheriting from `object`. Alternatively, you can call the `__init__` method of the superclass directly from an old-style class using: `BaseClass.__init__(...)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4692979Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4693066Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4693184Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4694982Z +               'This example shows a function that processes a sequence of lists of numbers. It prints out the largest element from each of the lists. In the first version, the variable `x` gets overwritten by the inner loop, resulting in the wrong output. In the second function, the error has been fixed by renaming the inner loop variable to stop it overwriting the outer loop variable.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4696505Z -               'In the following example, `PythonModule` is an old-style class as it inherits from another old-style class. If the `_ModuleIteratorHelper` class cannot be converted into a new-style class, then the call to `super()` must be replaced. The `PythonModule2` class demonstrates the correct way to call a superclass from an old-style class.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4696591Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4696674Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4696779Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4696944Z +               'def largest_elements(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4697077Z +               '    for x in l:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4697217Z +               '        maxnum = 0\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4697361Z +               '        for x in x:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4697535Z +               '            maxnum = max(x, maxnum)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4697882Z +               '        # The outer loop variable x has now been overwritten by the inner loop.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4698161Z +               '        print "The largest element in the list", x, "is", maxnum\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4698683Z -               "class PythonModule(_ModuleIteratorHelper): # '_ModuleIteratorHelper' and 'PythonModule' are old-style classes\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4698770Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4698937Z -               '    # class definitions ....\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699016Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699193Z +               'def largest_elements_correct(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699458Z +               '    for x in l:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699602Z +               '        maxnum = 0\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699743Z +               '        for y in x:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4699918Z +               '            maxnum = max(y, maxnum)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4700192Z +               '        print "The largest element in the list", x, "is", maxnum\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4700425Z -               '    def walkModules(self, importPackages=False):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4700643Z -               '        if importPackages and self.isPackage():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4700796Z -               '            self.load()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4701275Z -               '        return super(PythonModule, self).walkModules(importPackages=importPackages) # super() will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4701355Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4701441Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4701896Z -               'class PythonModule2(_ModuleIteratorHelper): # call to super replaced with direct call to class\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4701987Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4702154Z -               '    # class definitions ....\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4702369Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4702595Z -               '    def walkModules(self, importPackages=False):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4702816Z -               '        if importPackages and self.isPackage():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4702962Z -               '            self.load()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4703471Z -               '        return _ModuleIteratorHelper.__init__(PythonModule, self).walkModules(importPackages=importPackages)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4703567Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4703655Z                 '```\n' +
