2026-10-20T21:06:14.4711908Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4712023Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4712484Z +             text: 'Iteration over either a string or a sequence in the same loop can cause errors that are hard to find.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4712978Z -             text: 'Class definitions of equality and inequality operators may be inconsistent.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4713061Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4713142Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4713409Z +             markdown: '# Iterable can be either a string or a sequence\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4714779Z +               'The `for` statement is designed to allow you to iterate over the elements of a sequence or other iterable object. Strings in Python are iterable, and often used as such. However, they are also often considered, not as sequences of characters, but as atomic entities.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4715014Z -             markdown: '# Inconsistent equality and inequality\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4715939Z -               'In order to ensure the `==` and `!=` operators behave consistently as expected (i.e. they should be negations of each other), care should be taken when implementing the `__eq__` and `__ne__` special methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4716031Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4718112Z +               'One source of defects in Python is mistakenly iterating over a non-iterable object such as an integer. This sort of defect is easily detected as a `TypeError` will be raised. However, if a string is mistakenly used as the iterable in a `for` statement, which also receives other sequences (such as lists) then the code will iterate over the string one character at a time. This is probably not what the programmer intended and results in errors that are hard to find.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4718882Z -               'In Python 3, if the `__eq__` method is defined in a class while the `__ne__` is not, then the `!=` operator will automatically delegate to the `__eq__` method in the expected way.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4718970Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4720080Z -               'However, if the `__ne__` method is defined without a corresponding `__eq__` method, the `==` operator will still default to object identity (equivalent to the `is` operator), while the `!=` operator will use the `__ne__` method, which may be inconsistent.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4720168Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4721478Z -               'Additionally, if the `__ne__` method is defined on a superclass, and the subclass defines its own `__eq__` method without overriding the superclass `__ne__` method, the `!=` operator will use this superclass `__ne__` method, rather than automatically delegating to `__eq__`, which may be incorrect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4721574Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4721664Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4721797Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4722901Z +               'Since this defect usually indicates a logical error, it is not possible to give a general method for addressing the defect. However, adding a guard that checks that the iterator is not a string could be worthwhile.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4724205Z -               'Ensure that when an `__ne__` method is defined, the `__eq__` method is also defined, and their results are consistent. In most cases, the `__ne__` method does not need to be defined at all, as the default behavior is to delegate to `__eq__` and negate the result.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4724300Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4724385Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4724492Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4725368Z +               'In this example, the loop may iterate over `"Hello"` producing one character per line, as well as over `[ "Hello", "World" ]` It is likely that the programmer forgot to wrap the `"Hello"` in brackets.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726090Z -               'In the following example, `A` defines a `__ne__` method, but not an `__eq__` method. This leads to inconsistent results between equality and inequality operators.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726173Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726393Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726509Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726628Z -               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726795Z -               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4726941Z -               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727020Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727204Z +               '#Mistakenly mixed list and string\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727340Z +               'def greeting():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727485Z +               '    if is_global():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727668Z +               '        greet = [ "Hello", "World" ]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727787Z +               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4727943Z +               '        greet = "Hello"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728097Z +               '    for word in greet:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728234Z +               '        print(word)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728426Z -               '    # BAD: ne is defined, but not eq.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728596Z -               '    def __ne__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728774Z -               '        if not isinstance(other, A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4728959Z -               '            return NotImplemented \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729126Z -               '        return self.a != other.a\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729212Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729347Z +               '#Only use list\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729498Z +               'def fixed_greeting():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729642Z +               '    if is_global():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729819Z +               '        greet = [ "Hello", "World" ]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4729936Z +               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730101Z +               '        greet = [ "Hello" ]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730252Z +               '    for word in greet:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730387Z +               '        print(word)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730514Z -               'x = A(1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730631Z -               'y = A(1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4730717Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4731081Z -               'print(x == y) # Prints False (potentially unexpected - object identity is used)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4731243Z -               'print(x != y) # Prints False\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4731339Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4731429Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732144Z -               'In the following example, `C` defines an `__eq__` method, but its `__ne__` implementation is inherited from `B`, which is not consistent with the equality operation.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732231Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732325Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732447Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732730Z -               'class B:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4732899Z -               '    def __init__(self, b):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733034Z -               '        self.b = b \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733139Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733296Z -               '    def __eq__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733472Z -               '        return self.b == other.b \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733571Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4733726Z -               '    def __ne__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734028Z -               '        return self.b != other.b \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734132Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734259Z -               'class C(B):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734424Z -               '    def __init__(self, b, c):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734582Z -               '        super().__init__(b)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734725Z -               '        self.c = c \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4734824Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4735201Z -               '    # BAD: eq is defined, but != will use superclass ne method, which is not consistent\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4735502Z -               '    def __eq__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4735744Z -               '        return self.b == other.b and self.c == other.c \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4735838Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4736041Z -               'print(C(1,2) == C(1,3)) # Prints False \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4736319Z -               'print(C(1,2) != C(1,3)) # Prints False (potentially unexpected)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4736414Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4736511Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4736629Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4737607Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement), [object.__iter__](http://docs.python.org/2.7/reference/datamodel.html#object.__iter__).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4738129Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Iterator types](http://docs.python.org/dev/library/stdtypes.html#iterator-types).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4738805Z +               '* Scipy lecture notes: [Iterators, generator expressions and generators](http://scipy-lectures.github.io/advanced/advanced_python/#iterators).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4739046Z +             text: '# Iterable can be either a string or a sequence\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4740241Z +               'The `for` statement is designed to allow you to iterate over the elements of a sequence or other iterable object. Strings in Python are iterable, and often used as such. However, they are also often considered, not as sequences of characters, but as atomic entities.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4741130Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [object.__ne__](http://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__ne__), [Comparisons](http://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#comparisons).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4741343Z -             text: '# Inconsistent equality and inequality\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4742254Z -               'In order to ensure the `==` and `!=` operators behave consistently as expected (i.e. they should be negations of each other), care should be taken when implementing the `__eq__` and `__ne__` special methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4742344Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4744538Z +               'One source of defects in Python is mistakenly iterating over a non-iterable object such as an integer. This sort of defect is easily detected as a `TypeError` will be raised. However, if a string is mistakenly used as the iterable in a `for` statement, which also receives other sequences (such as lists) then the code will iterate over the string one character at a time. This is probably not what the programmer intended and results in errors that are hard to find.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4745449Z -               'In Python 3, if the `__eq__` method is defined in a class while the `__ne__` is not, then the `!=` operator will automatically delegate to the `__eq__` method in the expected way.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4745547Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4746657Z -               'However, if the `__ne__` method is defined without a corresponding `__eq__` method, the `==` operator will still default to object identity (equivalent to the `is` operator), while the `!=` operator will use the `__ne__` method, which may be inconsistent.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4746738Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4748046Z -               'Additionally, if the `__ne__` method is defined on a superclass, and the subclass defines its own `__eq__` method without overriding the superclass `__ne__` method, the `!=` operator will use this superclass `__ne__` method, rather than automatically delegating to `__eq__`, which may be incorrect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4748143Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4748233Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4748361Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4749328Z +               'Since this defect usually indicates a logical error, it is not possible to give a general method for addressing the defect. However, adding a guard that checks that the iterator is not a string could be worthwhile.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4750575Z -               'Ensure that when an `__ne__` method is defined, the `__eq__` method is also defined, and their results are consistent. In most cases, the `__ne__` method does not need to be defined at all, as the default behavior is to delegate to `__eq__` and negate the result.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4750663Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4750747Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4750857Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4751728Z +               'In this example, the loop may iterate over `"Hello"` producing one character per line, as well as over `[ "Hello", "World" ]` It is likely that the programmer forgot to wrap the `"Hello"` in brackets.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4752441Z -               'In the following example, `A` defines a `__ne__` method, but not an `__eq__` method. This leads to inconsistent results between equality and inequality operators.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4752526Z                 '\n' +
