2026-10-20T21:06:14.4806799Z +               'Creating a new exception object is no different from creating any other object. The exception needs to be raised to have an effect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4806980Z -             markdown: '# Incomplete ordering\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4808858Z -               'A class that implements the rich comparison operators (`__lt__`, `__gt__`, `__le__`, or `__ge__`) should ensure that all four comparison operations `<`, `<=`, `>`, and `>=` function as expected, consistent with expected mathematical rules. In Python 3, this is ensured by implementing one of `__lt__` or `__gt__`, and one of `__le__` or `__ge__`. If the ordering is not consistent with default equality, then `__eq__` should also be implemented.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4808955Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4809043Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4809169Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4809370Z +               'Insert a `raise` before the exception.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4809791Z -               'Ensure that at least one of `__lt__` or `__gt__` and at least one of `__le__` or `__ge__` is defined.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4809879Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4811127Z -               'The `functools.total_ordering` class decorator can be used to automatically implement all four comparison methods from a single one, which is typically the cleanest way to ensure all necessary comparison methods are implemented consistently.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4811218Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4811321Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4811441Z                 '## Example\n' +
