2026-10-20T21:06:14.4837007Z +             text: 'Nested loops in which the target variable is the same for each loop make the behavior of the loops difficult to understand.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4837459Z -             text: 'Overriding the class dictionary by declaring `__slots__` is not supported by old-style classes.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4837546Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4837633Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4837844Z +             markdown: '# Nested loops with same variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4839893Z +               'In Python variables have function-wide scope which means that if two variables have the same name in the same scope, they are in fact one variable. Consequently, nested loops in which the target variables have the same name in fact share a single variable. Such loops are difficult to understand as the inner loop will modify the target variable of the outer loop; this may be a typographical error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4840301Z -             markdown: '# `__slots__` in old-style class\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4842124Z -               'The ability to override the class dictionary using a `__slots__` declaration is supported only by new-style classes. When you add a `__slots__` declaration to an old-style class it just creates a class attribute called `__slots__`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4843671Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4844318Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4844809Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4846451Z +               'Carefully examine the code and check for possible errors, particularly considering what would happen if the inner or outer variable were renamed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4849214Z -               'If you want to override the dictionary for a class, then ensure that the class is a new-style class. You can convert an old-style class to a new-style class by inheriting from `object`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4850690Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4851080Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4851781Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4855115Z -               'In the following Python 2 example the `Point` class is an old-style class (no inheritance). The `__slots__` declaration in this class creates a class attribute called `__slots__`, the class dictionary is unaffected. The `Point2` class is a new-style class so the `__slots__` declaration causes special compact attributes to be created for each name in the slots list and saves space by not creating attribute dictionaries.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4856736Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4856963Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4857204Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4857494Z -               'class Point:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4857753Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4858039Z +               'for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4858381Z +               '    for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4858699Z +               '        pass\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4859145Z +               '    print (var) # Prints 2 2 2 not 0 1 2 as might be expected\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4859743Z -               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Incorrect: 'Point' is an old-style class.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4860445Z -               '                               # No slots are created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4861002Z -               '                               # Instances of Point have an attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4861394Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4861695Z -               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4862040Z -               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4862353Z -               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4862615Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4862828Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4863106Z -               'class Point2(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4863386Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4863986Z -               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Correct: 'Point2' is an new-style class\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4864545Z -               "                               # Two slots '_x' and '_y' are created.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4865109Z -               '                               # Instances of Point2 have no attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4865485Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4865766Z -               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4866093Z -               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4866396Z -               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4866653Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4866868Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4867077Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4867310Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4868040Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4869006Z +               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4869626Z +             text: '# Nested loops with same variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4871560Z +               'In Python variables have function-wide scope which means that if two variables have the same name in the same scope, they are in fact one variable. Consequently, nested loops in which the target variables have the same name in fact share a single variable. Such loops are difficult to understand as the inner loop will modify the target variable of the outer loop; this may be a typographical error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4873431Z -               '* Python Glossary: [New-style class](http://docs.python.org/glossary.html#term-new-style-class).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4874997Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [New-style and classic classes](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel.html#newstyle), [__slots__](http://docs.python.org/reference/datamodel.html#__slots__).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4876020Z -             text: '# `__slots__` in old-style class\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4877279Z -               'The ability to override the class dictionary using a `__slots__` declaration is supported only by new-style classes. When you add a `__slots__` declaration to an old-style class it just creates a class attribute called `__slots__`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4878218Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4878428Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4878678Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4879540Z +               'Carefully examine the code and check for possible errors, particularly considering what would happen if the inner or outer variable were renamed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4880956Z -               'If you want to override the dictionary for a class, then ensure that the class is a new-style class. You can convert an old-style class to a new-style class by inheriting from `object`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4881735Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4881939Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4882181Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4884274Z -               'In the following Python 2 example the `Point` class is an old-style class (no inheritance). The `__slots__` declaration in this class creates a class attribute called `__slots__`, the class dictionary is unaffected. The `Point2` class is a new-style class so the `__slots__` declaration causes special compact attributes to be created for each name in the slots list and saves space by not creating attribute dictionaries.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4885956Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4886173Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4886402Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4886692Z -               'class Point:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4886939Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4887207Z +               'for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4887546Z +               '    for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4887862Z +               '        pass\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4888287Z +               '    print (var) # Prints 2 2 2 not 0 1 2 as might be expected\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4888873Z -               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Incorrect: 'Point' is an old-style class.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4889379Z -               '                               # No slots are created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4889914Z -               '                               # Instances of Point have an attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4890285Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4890571Z -               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4890911Z -               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4891213Z -               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4891457Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4891673Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4891941Z -               'class Point2(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4892220Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4892625Z -               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Correct: 'Point2' is an new-style class\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4893151Z -               "                               # Two slots '_x' and '_y' are created.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4893700Z -               '                               # Instances of Point2 have no attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4894210Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4894494Z -               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4894816Z -               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4895113Z -               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4895367Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4895569Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4895779Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4896012Z                 '## References\n' +
