2026-10-20T21:06:14.4914042Z +             text: "Using a non-iterable as the object in a 'for' loop causes a TypeError."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4915199Z -             text: 'When a class subclasses multiple base classes and more than one base class defines the same attribute, attribute overriding may result in unexpected behavior by instances of this class.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4915977Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4916165Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4916478Z +             markdown: '# Non-iterable used in for loop\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4917781Z +               'The `for` statement is designed to allow you to iterate over the elements of a sequence or other iterable object. If a non-iterable object is used in a `for` statement (`for var in object:`) then a TypeError will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4918805Z -             markdown: '# Conflicting attributes in base classes\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4920742Z -               'When a class subclasses multiple base classes, attribute lookup is performed from left to right amongst the base classes. This form of attribute lookup is called "method resolution order" and is a solution to the [diamond inheritance problem](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiple_inheritance#The_diamond_problem) where several base classes override a method in a shared superclass.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4922182Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4924612Z -               'Unfortunately, this means that if more than one base class defines the same attribute, the leftmost base class will effectively override the attribute of the rightmost base class, even though the leftmost base class is not a subclass of the rightmost base class. Unless the methods in question are designed for inheritance, using `super`, then this implicit overriding may not be the desired behavior. Even if it is the desired behavior it makes the code hard to understand and maintain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4926563Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4926776Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4927019Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4927759Z +               'Since this defect usually indicates a logical error, it is not possible to give a general method for addressing the defect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4928575Z -               'There are a number of ways that might be used to address this issue:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4928969Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4929433Z -               '* Override the attribute in the subclass to implement the correct behavior.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4930199Z -               '* Modify the class hierarchy and move equivalent or redundant methods to a common super class.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4930987Z -               '* Modify the method hierarchy, breaking up complex methods into constituent parts.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4931562Z -               '* Use delegation rather than inheritance.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4931879Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4932111Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4932983Z +               'In this example, the loop may attempt to iterate over `None`, which is not an iterable. It is likely that the programmer forgot to test for `None` before the loop.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4935363Z -               'In this example the class `ThreadingTCPServer` inherits from `ThreadingMixIn` and from `TCPServer`. However, both these classes implement `process_request` which means that `ThreadingTCPServer` will inherit `process_request` from `ThreadingMixIn`. Consequently, the implementation of `process_request` in `TCPServer` will be ignored, which may not be the correct behavior.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4936780Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4936981Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4937201Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4937430Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4937712Z -               'class TCPServer(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4938007Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4938389Z -               '    def process_request(self, request, client_address):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4938851Z -               '        self.do_work(request, client_address)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4939260Z -               '        self.shutdown_request(request)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4939549Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4939852Z +               'def illegal_for_loop(seq = None):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4940188Z +               '    for x in seq:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4940486Z +               '        print (x)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4940746Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4941027Z -               'class ThreadingMixIn:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4941607Z -               '    """Mix-in class to handle each request in a new thread."""\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4941975Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4942338Z -               '    def process_request(self, request, client_address):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4942827Z -               '        """Start a new thread to process the request."""\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4943431Z -               '        t = threading.Thread(target = self.do_work, args = (request, client_address))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4944076Z -               '        t.daemon = self.daemon_threads\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4944420Z -               '        t.start()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4944663Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4945057Z -               'class ThreadingTCPServer(ThreadingMixIn, TCPServer): pass\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4945431Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4945633Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4946843Z -               'This can be fixed either by overriding the method, as shown in class `ThreadingTCPServerOverriding` or by ensuring that the functionality provided by the two base classes does not overlap, as shown in class `ThreadingTCPServerChangedHierarchy`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4947973Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4948180Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4948423Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4948666Z -               '   \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4949217Z -               '#Fixed by overriding. This does not change behavior, but makes it explicit and comprehensible.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4949915Z -               'class ThreadingTCPServerOverriding(ThreadingMixIn, TCPServer):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4950316Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4950690Z -               '    def process_request(self, request, client_address):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4951385Z -               '        #process_request forwards to do_work, so it is OK to call ThreadingMixIn.process_request directly\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4952107Z -               '        ThreadingMixIn.process_request(self, request, client_address)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4952545Z -               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4952783Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4953216Z -               '#Fixed by separating threading functionality from request handling.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4953678Z -               'class ThreadingMixIn:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4954191Z -               '    """Mix-in class to help with threads."""\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.4954514Z -               '\n' +
