2026-10-20T21:06:14.5385891Z +             markdown: "# Use of 'return' or 'yield' outside a function\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5386782Z +               'In Python, `return` and `yield` statements and the `yield from` expression can only be used within a function. Using them outside a function or a class method results in a `SyntaxError` at runtime.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5387042Z -             markdown: '# Result of integer division may be truncated\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5387615Z -               'In Python 2, the result of dividing two integers is silently truncated into an integer. This may lead to unexpected behavior.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5387788Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5387874Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5387994Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5388717Z +               'Using these elements outside a function or a class method usually indicates an error in the logic. Consequently, it is not possible to suggest a general fix.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5389874Z -               'If the division should never be truncated, add `from __future__ import division` to the beginning of the file. If the division should always be truncated, replace the division operator `/` with the truncated division operator `//`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5389961Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5390046Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5390164Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5392248Z +               'In this example, a `return` statement is used outside a class method in a class and a `yield` statement is used outside a function in a scope of a module which would result in a `SyntaxError` when running this code. In this example, the invalid class could be corrected by placing the `return` statement in a class method, or by refactoring the class into a function. The invalid `yield` statement could become part of a new generator function containing the `for` loop.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5393716Z -               'The first example shows a function for calculating the average of a sequence of numbers. When the function runs under Python 2, and the sequence contains only integers, an incorrect result may be returned because the result is truncated. The second example corrects this error by following the recommendation listed above.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5393939Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394031Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394220Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394450Z +               '# invalid class with return outside a function\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394627Z +               'class InvalidClass1(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394760Z +               '    if [1, 2, 3]:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5394915Z +               '        return "Exists"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395044Z -               '# Incorrect:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395135Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395371Z +               '# invalid statement with yield outside a function\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395510Z +               'for i in [1, 2, 3]:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395638Z +               '    yield i\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395778Z -               'def average(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5395937Z -               '    return sum(l) / len(l)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396022Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396231Z -               'print average([1.0, 2.0])  # Prints "1.5".\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396505Z -               'print average([1, 2])      # Prints "1", which is incorrect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396603Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396686Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396769Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5396894Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397016Z -               '# Correct:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397194Z -               'from __future__ import division\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397289Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397426Z -               'def average(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397585Z -               '    return sum(l) / len(l)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397674Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5397874Z -               'print average([1.0, 2.0])  # Prints "1.5".\n' +
