2026-10-20T21:06:14.5521630Z +             text: 'Overriding the class dictionary by declaring `__slots__` is not supported by old-style classes.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5522346Z -             text: `A string formatting operation, such as '"{0}: {1}, {2}".format(a,b)', where the number of values to be formatted is too few for the format string will raise an IndexError.`
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5522504Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5522586Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5522796Z +             markdown: '# `__slots__` in old-style class\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5523917Z +               'The ability to override the class dictionary using a `__slots__` declaration is supported only by new-style classes. When you add a `__slots__` declaration to an old-style class it just creates a class attribute called `__slots__`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5524149Z -             markdown: '# Too few arguments in formatting call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5525187Z -               'A formatting expression, that is an expression of the form `the_format.format(args)` or `format(the_format, args)`, must have sufficient arguments to match the format. Otherwise, an `IndexError` will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5525283Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5525367Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5525493Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5526290Z +               'If you want to override the dictionary for a class, then ensure that the class is a new-style class. You can convert an old-style class to a new-style class by inheriting from `object`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5526711Z -               'Either change the format to match the arguments, or ensure that there are sufficient arguments.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5526797Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5526880Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5526989Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5528790Z +               'In the following Python 2 example the `Point` class is an old-style class (no inheritance). The `__slots__` declaration in this class creates a class attribute called `__slots__`, the class dictionary is unaffected. The `Point2` class is a new-style class so the `__slots__` declaration causes special compact attributes to be created for each name in the slots list and saves space by not creating attribute dictionaries.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5529763Z -               'In the following example, only 2 arguments may be provided for the call to the `str.format` method, which is insufficient for the format string used. To fix this a third parameter should be provided on line 4.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5529848Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5529926Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530036Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530166Z +               'class Point:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530315Z -               'def unsafe_format():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530473Z -               '    the_format = "{} {} {}"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530633Z -               '    if unlikely_condition():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530827Z -               '        return the_format.format(1, 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5530950Z -               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5531142Z -               '        return the_format.format(1, 2, 3)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5531229Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5531536Z +               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Incorrect: 'Point' is an old-style class.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5531745Z +               '                               # No slots are created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532052Z +               '                               # Instances of Point have an attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532138Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532302Z +               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532440Z +               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532570Z +               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532663Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532759Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532906Z +               'class Point2(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5532999Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5533299Z +               "    __slots__ = [ '_x', '_y' ] # Correct: 'Point2' is an new-style class\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5533544Z +               "                               # Two slots '_x' and '_y' are created.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534042Z +               '                               # Instances of Point2 have no attribute dictionary.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534141Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534300Z +               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534439Z +               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534567Z +               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534662Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534750Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534829Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5534952Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5535529Z +               '* Python Glossary: [New-style class](http://docs.python.org/glossary.html#term-new-style-class).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5536388Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [New-style and classic classes](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel.html#newstyle), [__slots__](http://docs.python.org/reference/datamodel.html#__slots__).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5536586Z +             text: '# `__slots__` in old-style class\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5537580Z +               'The ability to override the class dictionary using a `__slots__` declaration is supported only by new-style classes. When you add a `__slots__` declaration to an old-style class it just creates a class attribute called `__slots__`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5538097Z -               '* Python Library Reference: [String Formatting.](https://docs.python.org/2/library/string.html#string-formatting)\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5538308Z -             text: '# Too few arguments in formatting call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5539220Z -               'A formatting expression, that is an expression of the form `the_format.format(args)` or `format(the_format, args)`, must have sufficient arguments to match the format. Otherwise, an `IndexError` will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5539367Z                 '\n' +
