2026-10-20T21:06:14.5624068Z +             markdown: '# Incomplete ordering\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5625868Z +               'A class that implements the rich comparison operators (`__lt__`, `__gt__`, `__le__`, or `__ge__`) should ensure that all four comparison operations `<`, `<=`, `>`, and `>=` function as expected, consistent with expected mathematical rules. In Python 3, this is ensured by implementing one of `__lt__` or `__gt__`, and one of `__le__` or `__ge__`. If the ordering is not consistent with default equality, then `__eq__` should also be implemented.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5626156Z -             markdown: '# Comparison using is when operands support `__eq__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5628071Z -               'When you compare two values using the `is` or `is not` operator, it is the object identities of the two values that is tested rather than their equality. If the class of either of the values in the comparison redefines equality then the `is` operator may return `False` even though the objects compare as equal. Equality is defined by the `__eq__` or, in Python2, `__cmp__` method. To compare two objects for equality, use the `==` or `!=` operator instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5628231Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5628315Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5628437Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5628842Z +               'Ensure that at least one of `__lt__` or `__gt__` and at least one of `__le__` or `__ge__` is defined.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5629372Z -               'When you want to compare the value of two literals, use the comparison operator `==` or `!=` in place of `is` or `is not`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5629456Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5630516Z +               'The `functools.total_ordering` class decorator can be used to automatically implement all four comparison methods from a single one, which is typically the cleanest way to ensure all necessary comparison methods are implemented consistently.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5631001Z -               'If the uniqueness property or performance are important then use an object that does not redefine equality.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5631087Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5631166Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5631277Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5632302Z +               'In the following example, only the `__lt__` operator has been implemented, which would lead to unexpected errors if the `<=` or `>=` operators are used on `A` instances. The `__le__` method should also be defined, as well as `__eq__` in this case.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5633219Z -               'In the first line of the following example the programmer tests the value of `value` against `DEFAULT` using the `is` operator. Unfortunately, this may fail when the function is called with the string `"default"`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5633305Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5634445Z -               'To function correctly, change the expression `value is DEFAULT` to `value == DEFAULT`. Alternatively, if the uniqueness property is desirable, then change the definition of `DEFAULT` to either of the alternatives below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5634528Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5634625Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5634732Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5634850Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5635013Z +               '    def __init__(self, i):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5635150Z +               '        self.i = i\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5635391Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5635707Z +               '    # BAD: le is not defined, so `A(1) <= A(2)` would result in an error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5635871Z +               '    def __lt__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636040Z +               '        return self.i < other.i\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636145Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636285Z -               'DEFAULT = "default"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636379Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636552Z -               'def get_color(name, fallback):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636698Z -               '    if name in COLORS:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5636856Z -               '        return COLORS[name]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637024Z -               '    elif fallback is DEFAULT:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637178Z -               '        return DEFAULT_COLOR\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637303Z -               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637455Z -               '        return fallback\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637549Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637676Z -               '#This works\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5637876Z -               'print (get_color("spam", "def" + "ault"))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638044Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638186Z -               '#But this does not\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638402Z -               'print (get_color("spam", "default-spam"[:7]))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638496Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638692Z -               '#To fix the above code change to object\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638828Z -               'DEFAULT = object()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5638922Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639105Z -               '#Or if you want better repr() output:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639262Z -               'class Default(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639357Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639502Z -               '    def __repr__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639656Z -               '        return "DEFAULT"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639751Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639887Z -               'DEFAULT = Default()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5639980Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5640070Z                 '```\n' +
