2026-10-20T21:06:14.5667563Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5667919Z +             text: 'Class definitions of equality and inequality operators may be inconsistent.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5668212Z -             text: 'Comparison of identical values, the intent of which is unclear.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5668425Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5668512Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5668747Z +             markdown: '# Inconsistent equality and inequality\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5669624Z +               'In order to ensure the `==` and `!=` operators behave consistently as expected (i.e. they should be negations of each other), care should be taken when implementing the `__eq__` and `__ne__` special methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5669841Z -             markdown: "# Maybe missing 'self' in comparison\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5670868Z -               'When two identical expressions are compared it is typically an indication of a mistake, since the Boolean value of the comparison will always be the same. Often, it can indicate that `self` has been omitted.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5670963Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5671759Z +               'In Python 3, if the `__eq__` method is defined in a class while the `__ne__` is not, then the `!=` operator will automatically delegate to the `__eq__` method in the expected way.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5671847Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5672908Z +               'However, if the `__ne__` method is defined without a corresponding `__eq__` method, the `==` operator will still default to object identity (equivalent to the `is` operator), while the `!=` operator will use the `__ne__` method, which may be inconsistent.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5673004Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5674402Z +               'Additionally, if the `__ne__` method is defined on a superclass, and the subclass defines its own `__eq__` method without overriding the superclass `__ne__` method, the `!=` operator will use this superclass `__ne__` method, rather than automatically delegating to `__eq__`, which may be incorrect.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5674582Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5674677Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5674806Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5675881Z +               'Ensure that when an `__ne__` method is defined, the `__eq__` method is also defined, and their results are consistent. In most cases, the `__ne__` method does not need to be defined at all, as the default behavior is to delegate to `__eq__` and negate the result.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5676942Z -               'It is never good practice to compare a value with itself. If `self` has been omitted, then insert it. If the constant behavior is indeed required, use the Boolean literals `True` or `False`, rather than encoding them obscurely as `x == x` or similar.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5677031Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5677108Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5677222Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5677909Z +               'In the following example, `A` defines a `__ne__` method, but not an `__eq__` method. This leads to inconsistent results between equality and inequality operators.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5677994Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678088Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678197Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678316Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678478Z +               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678618Z +               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678695Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5678885Z +               '    # BAD: ne is defined, but not eq.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679043Z +               '    def __ne__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679223Z +               '        if not isinstance(other, A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679402Z +               '            return NotImplemented \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679568Z +               '        return self.a != other.a\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679709Z -               'class Customer:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679792Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5679984Z +               'x = A(1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5680101Z +               'y = A(1)\n' +
