2026-10-20T21:06:14.5804982Z -             text: 'First argument to super() is not enclosing class'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805061Z             }
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805142Z           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805222Z           {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805340Z             defaultConfiguration: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805437Z               enabled: true,
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805510Z ...
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5805625Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5806094Z +             text: 'A duplicated call to a superclass `__del__` method may lead to class instances not be cleaned up properly.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5806497Z -             text: 'Comparison of constants is always constant, but is harder to read than a simple constant.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5806584Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5806784Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5807068Z +             markdown: '# Multiple calls to `__del__` during object destruction\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5808285Z +               'Python, unlike some other object-oriented languages such as Java, allows the developer complete freedom in when and how superclass finalizers are called during object finalization. However, the developer has responsibility for ensuring that objects are properly cleaned up.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5808470Z -             markdown: '# Comparison of constants\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5809477Z -               'When two constants are compared it is typically an indication of a mistake, since the Boolean value of the comparison will always be the same. In very old code this may be used to initialize `True` and `False`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5809571Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5810823Z +               'Objects with a `__del__` method (a finalizer) often hold resources such as file handles that need to be cleaned up. If a superclass finalizer is called multiple times, this may lead to errors such as closing an already closed file, and lead to objects not being cleaned up properly as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5810915Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5811256Z +               'There are a number of ways that a `__del__` method may be called more than once.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5811350Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5811751Z +               '* There may be more than one explicit call to the method in the hierarchy of `__del__` methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5812790Z +               '* In situations involving multiple inheritance, an finalization method may call the finalizers of each of its base types, which themselves both call the finalizer of a shared base type. (This is an example of the Diamond Inheritance problem)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5814466Z +               '* Another situation involving multiple inheritance arises when a subclass calls the `__del__` methods of each of its base classes, one of which calls `super().__del__`. This super call resolves to the next class in the Method Resolution Order (MRO) of the subclass, which may be another base class that already has its initializer explicitly called.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5814571Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5814692Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5815469Z +               'Ensure that each finalizer method is called exactly once during finalization. This can be ensured by calling `super().__del__` for each finalizer method in the inheritance chain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5816639Z -               'It is never good practice to compare a value with itself. If the constant behavior is indeed required, use the Boolean literals `True` or `False`, rather than encoding them obscurely as `1 == 1` or similar. If there is a mistake, ascertain the desired behavior and correct it.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5816722Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5816809Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5816923Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5818478Z +               'In the following example, there is a mixture of explicit calls to `__del__` and calls using `super()`, resulting in `Vehicle.__del__` being called twice. `FixedSportsCar.__del__` fixes this by using `super()` consistently with the other delete methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819221Z -               'In this example, old code uses `1==1` to initialize `__builtins__.True`. This code has been unnecessary on all versions of Python released since 2003 and can be deleted.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819312Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819398Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819511Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819596Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5819966Z +               '#Calling a method multiple times by using explicit calls when a base uses super()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820127Z +               'class Vehicle(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820363Z +               '     \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820510Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820688Z +               '        recycle(self.base_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820870Z +               '        super(Vehicle, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5820995Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821140Z +               'class Car(Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821235Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821382Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821551Z +               '        recycle(self.car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821855Z +               '        super(Car, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5821978Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822091Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822268Z +               'class SportsCar(Car, Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822365Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822584Z +               '    # BAD: Vehicle.__del__ will get called twice\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822736Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5822926Z +               '        recycle(self.sports_car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5823074Z +               '        Car.__del__(self)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5823236Z +               '        Vehicle.__del__(self)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5823353Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5823465Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5823747Z +               '# GOOD: super() is used ensuring each del method is called once.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824084Z +               'class FixedSportsCar(Car, Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824188Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824335Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824613Z +               '        recycle(self.sports_car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824803Z +               '        super(SportsCar, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5824907Z +               '     \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825170Z -               '#Interoperate with very old versions of Python (pre 2.3)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825274Z -               'try:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825394Z -               '    True\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825532Z -               'except NameError:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825694Z -               '    __builtins__.True = 1==1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825776Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825871Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5825956Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5826033Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5826155Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5826601Z +               '* Python Reference: [__del__](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__del__).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5827019Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [super](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#super).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5827556Z +               '* Python Glossary: [Method resolution order](https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-method-resolution-order).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5828037Z +               '* Wikipedia: [The Diamond Problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiple_inheritance#The_diamond_problem).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5828304Z +             text: '# Multiple calls to `__del__` during object destruction\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5829517Z +               'Python, unlike some other object-oriented languages such as Java, allows the developer complete freedom in when and how superclass finalizers are called during object finalization. However, the developer has responsibility for ensuring that objects are properly cleaned up.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5829992Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [Comparisons](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/expressions.html#not-in).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5830395Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-570](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/570.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5830870Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-571](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/571.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5831044Z -             text: '# Comparison of constants\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5831938Z -               'When two constants are compared it is typically an indication of a mistake, since the Boolean value of the comparison will always be the same. In very old code this may be used to initialize `True` and `False`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5832027Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5833385Z +               'Objects with a `__del__` method (a finalizer) often hold resources such as file handles that need to be cleaned up. If a superclass finalizer is called multiple times, this may lead to errors such as closing an already closed file, and lead to objects not being cleaned up properly as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5833471Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5833946Z +               'There are a number of ways that a `__del__` method may be called more than once.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5834051Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5834454Z +               '* There may be more than one explicit call to the method in the hierarchy of `__del__` methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5835501Z +               '* In situations involving multiple inheritance, an finalization method may call the finalizers of each of its base types, which themselves both call the finalizer of a shared base type. (This is an example of the Diamond Inheritance problem)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5836984Z +               '* Another situation involving multiple inheritance arises when a subclass calls the `__del__` methods of each of its base classes, one of which calls `super().__del__`. This super call resolves to the next class in the Method Resolution Order (MRO) of the subclass, which may be another base class that already has its initializer explicitly called.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5837158Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5837283Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5838059Z +               'Ensure that each finalizer method is called exactly once during finalization. This can be ensured by calling `super().__del__` for each finalizer method in the inheritance chain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5839237Z -               'It is never good practice to compare a value with itself. If the constant behavior is indeed required, use the Boolean literals `True` or `False`, rather than encoding them obscurely as `1 == 1` or similar. If there is a mistake, ascertain the desired behavior and correct it.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5839320Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5839407Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5839521Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5840586Z +               'In the following example, there is a mixture of explicit calls to `__del__` and calls using `super()`, resulting in `Vehicle.__del__` being called twice. `FixedSportsCar.__del__` fixes this by using `super()` consistently with the other delete methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5841314Z -               'In this example, old code uses `1==1` to initialize `__builtins__.True`. This code has been unnecessary on all versions of Python released since 2003 and can be deleted.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5841399Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5841477Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5841588Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5841670Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842031Z +               '#Calling a method multiple times by using explicit calls when a base uses super()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842186Z +               'class Vehicle(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842284Z +               '     \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842436Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842610Z +               '        recycle(self.base_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842797Z +               '        super(Vehicle, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5842989Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843134Z +               'class Car(Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843227Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843378Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843548Z +               '        recycle(self.car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843718Z +               '        super(Car, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5843954Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5844071Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5844245Z +               'class SportsCar(Car, Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5844343Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5844689Z +               '    # BAD: Vehicle.__del__ will get called twice\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5844850Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845042Z +               '        recycle(self.sports_car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845191Z +               '        Car.__del__(self)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845352Z +               '        Vehicle.__del__(self)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845469Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845581Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5845864Z +               '# GOOD: super() is used ensuring each del method is called once.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846048Z +               'class FixedSportsCar(Car, Vehicle):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846150Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846296Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846476Z +               '        recycle(self.sports_car_parts)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846667Z +               '        super(SportsCar, self).__del__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5846767Z +               '     \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847026Z -               '#Interoperate with very old versions of Python (pre 2.3)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847202Z -               'try:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847321Z -               '    True\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847459Z -               'except NameError:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847624Z -               '    __builtins__.True = 1==1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847703Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847797Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847884Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5847964Z                 '\n' +
