2026-10-20T21:06:14.5971474Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5972066Z +             text: 'Calling a method from `__init__` that is overridden by a subclass may result in a partially initialized instance being observed.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5972519Z -             text: "Regular expressions containing a dollar '$' in the middle cannot be matched, whatever the input."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5972600Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5972772Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5973027Z +             markdown: '# `__init__` method calls overridden method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5976110Z +               "When initializing an instance of the class in the class' `__init__` method, calls tha are made using the instance may receive an instance of the class that is not yet fully initialized. When a method called in an initializer is overridden in a subclass, the subclass method receives the instance in a potentially unexpected state. Fields that would be initialized after the call, including potentially in the subclass' `__init__` method, will not be initialized. This may lead to runtime errors, as well as make the code more difficult to maintain, as future changes may not be aware of which fields would not be initialized.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5976378Z -             markdown: '# Unmatchable dollar in regular expression\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5977846Z -               'A dollar assertion `$` in a regular expression only matches at the end of the input, or (for multi-line regular expressions) at the end of a line. If it is followed by a pattern that must match a non-empty sequence of (non-newline) input characters, it cannot possibly match, rendering the entire regular expression unmatchable.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5977938Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5978018Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5978142Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5979419Z +               "If possible, refactor the initializer method such that initialization is complete before calling any overridden methods. For helper methods used as part of initialization, avoid overriding them, and instead call any additional logic required in the subclass' `__init__` method.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5979707Z -               'Examine the regular expression to find and correct any typos.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5979858Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5980532Z +               'If the overridden method does not depend on the instance `self`, and only on its class, consider making it a `@classmethod` or `@staticmethod` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5980617Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5982068Z +               'If calling an overridden method is absolutely required, consider marking it as an internal method (by using an `_` prefix) to discourage external users of the library from overriding it and observing partially initialized state, and ensure that the fact it is called during initialization is mentioned in the documentation.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5982165Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5982254Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5982369Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5983371Z +               'In the following case, the `__init__` method of `Super` calls the `set_up` method that is overridden by `Sub`. This results in `Sub.set_up` being called with a partially initialized instance of `Super` which may be unexpected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5988900Z -               'In the following example, the regular expression `r"\\.\\(\\w+$\\)"` cannot match any string, since it contains a dollar assertion followed by an escape sequence that matches a closing parenthesis.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989005Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989482Z -               'The second regular expression, `r"\\.\\(\\w+\\)$"`, has the dollar at the end and will work as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989571Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989711Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989864Z +               'class Super(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5989955Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5990139Z +               '    def __init__(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5990310Z +               '        self._state = "Not OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5990850Z +               '        self.set_up(arg) # BAD: This method is overridden, so `Sub.set_up` receives a partially initialized instance.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991017Z +               '        self._state = "OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991248Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991421Z +               '    def set_up(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991576Z +               '        "Do some setup"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991711Z +               '        self.a = 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991811Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5991950Z +               'class Sub(Super):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992047Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992213Z +               '    def __init__(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992373Z +               '        super().__init__(arg)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992687Z +               '        self.important_state = "OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992788Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5992943Z +               '    def set_up(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5993106Z +               '        super().set_up(arg)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5993267Z +               '        "Do some more setup"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5993739Z +               '        # BAD: at this point `self._state` is set to `"Not OK"`, and `self.important_state` is not initialized.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5994119Z +               '        if self._state == "OK":\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5994286Z +               '            self.b = self.a + 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5994383Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5994483Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5994964Z +               'In the following case, the initialization methods are separate between the superclass and the subclass.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995061Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995155Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995269Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995422Z +               'class Super(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995546Z -               'import re\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5995934Z -               '#Regular expression that includes a dollar, but not at the end.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5996142Z -               'matcher = re.compile(r"\\.\\(\\w+$\\)")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5996225Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5996389Z +               '    def __init__(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5996555Z +               '        self._state = "Not OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997229Z +               "        self.super_set_up(arg) # GOOD: This isn't overriden. Instead, additional setup the subclass needs is called by the subclass' `__init__ method.`\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997385Z +               '        self._state = "OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997541Z -               'def find_it(filename):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997715Z -               '    if matcher.match(filename):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997872Z -               '        print("Found it!")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5997955Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998125Z +               '    def super_set_up(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998277Z +               '        "Do some setup"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998420Z +               '        self.a = 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998512Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998606Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998745Z +               'class Sub(Super):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5998841Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999006Z +               '    def __init__(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999166Z +               '        super().__init__(arg)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999351Z +               '        self.sub_set_up(self, arg)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999536Z +               '        self.important_state = "OK"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999628Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999725Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.5999894Z +               '    def sub_set_up(self, arg):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000057Z +               '        "Do some more setup"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000228Z +               '        if self._state == "OK":\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000390Z +               '            self.b = self.a + 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000694Z -               '#Regular expression anchored to end of input.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000910Z -               'fixed_matcher = re.compile(r"\\.\\(\\w+\\)$")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6000994Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6001079Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6001196Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6002431Z +               '* CERT Secure Coding: [ Rule MET05-J](https://www.securecoding.cert.org/confluence/display/java/MET05-J.+Ensure+that+constructors+do+not+call+overridable+methods). Reference discusses Java but is applicable to object oriented programming in many languages.\n' +
