2026-10-20T21:06:14.6227161Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6227722Z +             text: 'Defining an attribute in a superclass method with a name that matches a subclass method, hides the method in the subclass.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6228106Z -             text: 'A lambda is used that calls through to a function without modifying any parameters'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6228190Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6228277Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6228541Z +             markdown: '# Superclass attribute shadows subclass method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6230306Z +               "When an object has an attribute that shares its name with a method on the object's class (or another class attribute), the instance attribute is prioritized during attribute lookup, shadowing the method. If a method on a subclass is shadowed by an attribute on a superclass in this way, this may lead to unexpected results or errors, as this shadowing behavior is nonlocal and may be unintended.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6230485Z -             markdown: '# Unnecessary lambda\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6231454Z -               'A lambda that calls a function without modifying any of its parameters is unnecessary. Python functions are first class objects and can be passed around in the same way as the resulting lambda.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6231539Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6231626Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6231751Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6232584Z +               "Ensure method names on subclasses don't conflict with attribute names on superclasses, and rename one. If the shadowing behavior is intended, ensure this is explicit in the superclass.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6232927Z -               'Remove the lambda, use the function directly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6233015Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6233095Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6233214Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6234117Z +               'In the following example, the `_foo` attribute of class `A` shadows the method `_foo` of class `B`. Calls to `B()._foo()` will result in a `TypeError`, as `3` will be called instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6234661Z -               'In this example a lambda is used unnecessarily in order to pass a method as an argument to ` call_with_x_squared`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6234754Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6234845Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6234955Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235090Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235249Z +               '    def __init__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235403Z +               '        self._foo = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235548Z -               'import math\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235631Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6235765Z +               'class B(A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236122Z +               '    # BAD: _foo is shadowed by attribute A._foo\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236269Z +               '    def _foo(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236416Z +               '        return 2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236612Z -               'def call_with_x_squared(x, function):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236746Z -               '    x = x*x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236907Z -               '    return function(x)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6236992Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6237092Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6237381Z -               'print call_with_x_squared(2, lambda x: math.factorial(x))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6237469Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6238559Z +               'In the following example, the behavior of the `default` attribute being shadowed to allow for customization during initialization is intended in within the superclass `A`. Overriding `default` in the subclass `B` is then OK.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6238997Z -               'This is not necessary as methods can be passed directly. They behave as callable objects.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239083Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239173Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239289Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239412Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239627Z +               '    def __init__(self, default_func=None):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6239815Z +               '        if default_func is not None:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240016Z +               '            self.default = default_func \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240152Z -               'import math\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240236Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240599Z +               '    # GOOD: The shadowing behavior is explicitly intended in the superclass.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240759Z +               '    def default(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6240899Z +               '        return []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6241008Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6241146Z +               'class B(A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6241245Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6241895Z +               '    # Subclasses may override the method `default`, which will still be shadowed by the attribute `default` if it is set.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6242262Z +               '    # As this is part of the expected behavior of the superclass, this is fine. \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6242416Z +               '    def default(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6242558Z +               '        return {}\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6242657Z +               '```\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6242913Z +             text: '# Superclass attribute shadows subclass method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245029Z +               "When an object has an attribute that shares its name with a method on the object's class (or another class attribute), the instance attribute is prioritized during attribute lookup, shadowing the method. If a method on a subclass is shadowed by an attribute on a superclass in this way, this may lead to unexpected results or errors, as this shadowing behavior is nonlocal and may be unintended.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245250Z -               'def call_with_x_squared(x, function):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245379Z -               '    x = x*x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245537Z -               '    return function(x)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245622Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245854Z -               'print call_with_x_squared(2, math.factorial)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6245964Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6246047Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6246189Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6246594Z -               '* Python: [lambdas](http://docs.python.org/2.7/reference/expressions.html#lambda).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6246758Z -             text: '# Unnecessary lambda\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6247692Z -               'A lambda that calls a function without modifying any of its parameters is unnecessary. Python functions are first class objects and can be passed around in the same way as the resulting lambda.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6247862Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6247958Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6248086Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6248977Z +               "Ensure method names on subclasses don't conflict with attribute names on superclasses, and rename one. If the shadowing behavior is intended, ensure this is explicit in the superclass.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6249204Z -               'Remove the lambda, use the function directly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6249293Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6249375Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6249493Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6250325Z +               'In the following example, the `_foo` attribute of class `A` shadows the method `_foo` of class `B`. Calls to `B()._foo()` will result in a `TypeError`, as `3` will be called instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6250869Z -               'In this example a lambda is used unnecessarily in order to pass a method as an argument to ` call_with_x_squared`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6250962Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6251051Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6251160Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6251292Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6251453Z +               '    def __init__(self):\n' +
