2026-10-20T21:06:14.6473458Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6473571Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6474183Z +             text: 'Module uses member of cyclically imported module, which can lead to failure at import time.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6474664Z -             text: 'Defining special methods for slicing has been deprecated since Python 2.0.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6474753Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6474839Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6475031Z +             markdown: '# Module-level cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6476704Z +               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement. If all imports in a cyclic import occur at module level, then a module will be imported when it is part way through its initialization. This may rest in surprising errors, as parts of the module being imported may not yet exist.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6476895Z -             markdown: '# Deprecated slice method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6477548Z -               'The `__getslice__`, `__setslice__` and `__delslice__` methods have been deprecated since Python 2.0. In general, no class should implement these methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6477635Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6478655Z +               'In addition to the possible errors, cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6480347Z -               'The only exceptions to this rule are classes that inherit from `list` and override `__getitem__`, `__setitem__` or `__delitem__`. Since `list` implements the slicing methods any class inheriting from `list` must implement the the slicing methods to ensure correct behavior of `__getitem__`, `__setitem__` and `__delitem__`. These exceptions to the rule will not be treated as violations.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6480439Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6480523Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6480640Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6482059Z +               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6482516Z -               'Delete the slicing method. Any functionality should be moved to the equivalent `__xxxitem__` method:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6482598Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6482841Z -               '* `__getslice__` should be replaced with `__getitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6483081Z -               '* `__setslice__` should be replaced with `__setitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6483308Z -               '* `__delslice__` should be replaced with `__delitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6483392Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6483510Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6484152Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6484471Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6485010Z +               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6485275Z +             text: '# Module-level cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6486929Z +               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement. If all imports in a cyclic import occur at module level, then a module will be imported when it is part way through its initialization. This may rest in surprising errors, as parts of the module being imported may not yet exist.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6487910Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [ Additional methods for emulation of sequence types](https://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel.html#additional-methods-for-emulation-of-sequence-types).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6488082Z -             text: '# Deprecated slice method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6488734Z -               'The `__getslice__`, `__setslice__` and `__delslice__` methods have been deprecated since Python 2.0. In general, no class should implement these methods.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6488826Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6489847Z +               'In addition to the possible errors, cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6491470Z -               'The only exceptions to this rule are classes that inherit from `list` and override `__getitem__`, `__setitem__` or `__delitem__`. Since `list` implements the slicing methods any class inheriting from `list` must implement the the slicing methods to ensure correct behavior of `__getitem__`, `__setitem__` and `__delitem__`. These exceptions to the rule will not be treated as violations.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6491558Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6491643Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6491829Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6493241Z +               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6493699Z -               'Delete the slicing method. Any functionality should be moved to the equivalent `__xxxitem__` method:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6493898Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6494141Z -               '* `__getslice__` should be replaced with `__getitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6494380Z -               '* `__setslice__` should be replaced with `__setitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6494607Z -               '* `__delslice__` should be replaced with `__delitem__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6494694Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6494810Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6495322Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6495634Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6496159Z +               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6497004Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [ Additional methods for emulation of sequence types](https://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel.html#additional-methods-for-emulation-of-sequence-types).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6497086Z             },
