2026-10-20T21:06:14.6570855Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6570965Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6571312Z +             text: 'Module forms part of an import cycle, thereby indirectly importing itself.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6571788Z -             text: "The `__iter__` method returns a non-iterator which, if used in a 'for' loop, would raise a 'TypeError'."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6571867Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6571954Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6572120Z +             markdown: '# Cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6572826Z +               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6573078Z -             markdown: '# `__iter__` method returns a non-iterator\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6573382Z -               'The `__iter__` method of a class should always return an iterator.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6573466Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6574489Z +               'Cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6575339Z -               'Iterators must implement both `__next__` and `__iter__` for Python 3, or both `next` and `__iter__` for Python 2. The `__iter__` method of the iterator must return the iterator object itself.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6575426Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6576186Z -               'Iteration in Python relies on this behavior and attempting to iterate over an instance of a class with an incorrect `__iter__` method can raise a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6576277Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6576372Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6576582Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6578140Z +               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6578525Z -               'Make sure the value returned by `__iter__` implements the full iterator protocol.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6578614Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6578704Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6578954Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6579520Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6579851Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6580437Z +               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6580593Z +             text: '# Cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6581336Z +               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6581474Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6582854Z -               'In this example, we have implemented our own version of `range`, extending the normal functionality with the ability to skip some elements by using the `skip` method. However, the iterator `MyRangeIterator` does not fully implement the iterator protocol (namely it is missing `__iter__`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6583050Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6584105Z +               'Cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6585238Z -               "Iterating over the elements in the range seems to work on the surface, for example the code `x = sum(my_range)` gives the expected result. However, if we run `sum(iter(my_range))` we get a `TypeError: 'MyRangeIterator' object is not iterable`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6585327Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6585936Z -               'If we try to skip some elements using our custom method, for example `y = sum(my_range.skip({6,9}))`, this also raises a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6586029Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6586178Z +               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6587730Z +               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6588056Z -               'The fix is to implement the `__iter__` method in `MyRangeIterator`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6588144Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6588229Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6588369Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6588925Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6589254Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6589830Z +               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6589920Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6590060Z +           id: 'py/cyclic-import',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6590214Z +           name: 'py/cyclic-import',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6590405Z +           properties: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6590593Z +             'problem.severity': 'recommendation',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6590738Z +             'sub-severity': 'low',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591142Z +             description: 'Module forms part of an import cycle, thereby indirectly importing itself.',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591289Z +             id: 'py/cyclic-import',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591419Z +             kind: 'problem',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591557Z +             name: 'Cyclic import',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591694Z +             precision: 'high',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6591788Z +             tags: [
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592042Z +               'reliability',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592188Z +               'maintainability',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592294Z +               'modularity'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592383Z +             ]
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592470Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592603Z +           shortDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592745Z +             text: 'Cyclic import'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592824Z +           }
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592909Z +         },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6592990Z +         {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593131Z +           defaultConfiguration: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593242Z +             enabled: true,
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593350Z +             level: 'note'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593428Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593563Z +           fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593723Z +             text: 'A module imports itself'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6593921Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6594029Z +           help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6594220Z +             markdown: '# Module imports itself\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6594974Z +               'There is no need for a module to import itself. A module importing itself may lead to errors as the module may be in an incomplete state when imported by itself.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595184Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595349Z -               'class MyRange(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595547Z -               '    def __init__(self, low, high):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595701Z -               '        self.low = low\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595857Z -               '        self.high = high\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6595946Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6596098Z -               '    def __iter__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6596345Z -               '        return MyRangeIterator(self.low, self.high)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6596432Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6596575Z +               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6597209Z +               'Remove the import statement. Convert all expressions of the form `mod.name` where "mod" is the name of the current module to `name`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6597385Z -               '    def skip(self, to_skip):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6597668Z -               '        return MyRangeIterator(self.low, self.high, to_skip)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6597761Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6597952Z -               'class MyRangeIterator(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598170Z -               '    def __init__(self, low, high, skip=None):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598335Z -               '        self.current = low\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598498Z -               '        self.high = high\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598651Z -               '        self.skip = skip\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598739Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6598872Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6599498Z +               'In this example the module, `ModuleImportsItself` imports itself and has an expression referencing the module it is in as well.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6599655Z -               '    def __next__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6599846Z -               '        if self.current >= self.high:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6600030Z -               '            raise StopIteration\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6600210Z -               '        to_return = self.current\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6600449Z -               '        self.current += 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6600682Z -               '        if self.skip and to_return in self.skip:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6600868Z -               '            return self.__next__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601021Z -               '        return to_return\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601113Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601349Z -               '    # Problem is fixed by uncommenting these lines\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601513Z -               '    # def __iter__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601665Z -               '    #     return self\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6601870Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6605512Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6605756Z +               'import ModuleImportsItself\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6605954Z -               'my_range = MyRange(0,10)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6606128Z -               'x = sum(my_range) # x = 45\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6606538Z -               "y = sum(my_range.skip({6,9})) # TypeError: 'MyRangeIterator' object is not iterable\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6606628Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6606785Z +               'def factorial(n):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6606920Z +               '    if n <= 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607067Z +               '        return 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607328Z +               '    return n * ModuleImportsItself.factorial(n - 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607418Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607725Z +               'The import can be removed and the reference can be corrected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607819Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6607958Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6608537Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [object.__iter__](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__iter__).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6609203Z -               '* Python Standard Library: [Iterator Types](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#typeiter).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6609445Z -             text: '# `__iter__` method returns a non-iterator\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6609765Z -               'The `__iter__` method of a class should always return an iterator.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6609856Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6609985Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6610856Z -               'Iterators must implement both `__next__` and `__iter__` for Python 3, or both `next` and `__iter__` for Python 2. The `__iter__` method of the iterator must return the iterator object itself.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6610945Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6611095Z +               'def factorial(n):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6611232Z +               '    if n <= 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6611367Z +               '        return 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6611553Z +               '    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6611658Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6612413Z -               'Iteration in Python relies on this behavior and attempting to iterate over an instance of a class with an incorrect `__iter__` method can raise a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6612505Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6612645Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6612972Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6613148Z +             text: '# Module imports itself\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6614112Z +               'There is no need for a module to import itself. A module importing itself may lead to errors as the module may be in an incomplete state when imported by itself.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6614204Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6614306Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6614437Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6615062Z +               'Remove the import statement. Convert all expressions of the form `mod.name` where "mod" is the name of the current module to `name`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6615543Z -               'Make sure the value returned by `__iter__` implements the full iterator protocol.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6615627Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6615716Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6615834Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6616450Z +               'In this example the module, `ModuleImportsItself` imports itself and has an expression referencing the module it is in as well.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6617946Z -               'In this example, we have implemented our own version of `range`, extending the normal functionality with the ability to skip some elements by using the `skip` method. However, the iterator `MyRangeIterator` does not fully implement the iterator protocol (namely it is missing `__iter__`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6618041Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6619161Z -               "Iterating over the elements in the range seems to work on the surface, for example the code `x = sum(my_range)` gives the expected result. However, if we run `sum(iter(my_range))` we get a `TypeError: 'MyRangeIterator' object is not iterable`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6619247Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6619382Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6619556Z +               'import ModuleImportsItself\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6620172Z -               'If we try to skip some elements using our custom method, for example `y = sum(my_range.skip({6,9}))`, this also raises a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6620262Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6620410Z +               'def factorial(n):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6620549Z +               '    if n <= 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6620691Z +               '        return 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621011Z +               '    return n * ModuleImportsItself.factorial(n - 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621116Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621417Z +               'The import can be removed and the reference can be corrected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621728Z -               'The fix is to implement the `__iter__` method in `MyRangeIterator`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621818Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6621908Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622017Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622178Z -               'class MyRange(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622363Z -               '    def __init__(self, low, high):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622520Z -               '        self.low = low\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622680Z -               '        self.high = high\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622766Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6622913Z +               'def factorial(n):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623047Z +               '    if n <= 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623184Z +               '        return 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623366Z +               '    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623469Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623628Z -               '    def __iter__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6623992Z -               '        return MyRangeIterator(self.low, self.high)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6624079Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6624219Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6624542Z +               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6624623Z +           },
